>
  <p>
    Red-teaming in artificial intelligence has historically mirrored cybersecurity: a small, specialized, 
    internal technical cohort attempting to probe model boundaries and extract adversarial prompts.
  </p>

  <p>
    However, generative models are not closed technical systems; they are socio-technical interfaces. 
    A small engineering team based in Silicon Valley cannot anticipate how an AI system will interact with 
    linguistic nuances, regional cultural sensitivities, or socio-economic edge cases in Southeast Asia or Latin America.
  </p>

  <h2>Why Closed Red-Teaming Fails</h2>
  <p>
    Homogeneous red teams suffer from collective blind spots. When evaluating jailbreaks, algorithmic bias, 
    or harmful hallucination, the test vectors reflect the lived experiences and biases of the testers. 
    Without diverse participation, major harms remain undiscovered until after mass deployment.
  </p>

  <h2>The Participatory Red-Teaming Framework</h2>
  <p>
    By adapting community architecture principles, organizations can construct participatory red-teaming programs:
  </p>

  <h3>1. Scoped Cohort Recruitment</h3>
  <p>
    Engaging domain specialists, grassroots community leaders, linguists, and regional practitioners rather than 
    relying solely on computer scientists.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Structured Feedback Protocols</h3>
  <p>
    Adversarial testing must translate into machine-readable and policy-actionable outputs. We build taxonomy matrices 
    so qualitative community feedback directly informs model fine-tuning and system prompt boundaries.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Closing the Governance Loop</h3>
  <p>
    True participatory governance requires transparency. Community participants must see how their discoveries 
    resulted in guardrail adjustments, creating an enduring culture of collective safety stewardship.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
